## TICKET 2 (P0) — Aerosil all-zero: Section E2's reference-column test fires on VALUE match, not on column LABEL

**Confirmed root cause.** The Evonik source table has two numeric columns for the same elements: an "ICH Q3D [µg/g]" reference column (0.5, 0.5, 1.5, 3, 5, 10, 20 for Cd/Pb/As/Hg/Co/V/Ni) and an "AEROSIL® 200 Pharma [µg/g]" actual-grade result column that legitimately reads the SAME bounds (`<0.5, <0.5, <1.5, <3, <5, <10, <20`), because Evonik controls this product at the guideline limit across all its grades — a real, common, non-fabricated situation (identical in principle to Trilaciclib's API spec sitting at the ICH limit, which we already established must be kept).

`make_ei_prompt` Section E2 currently instructs: *"when EVERY element in a column carries exactly that element's ICH Q3D Option-1 permitted concentration... the column reproduces the guideline table and is a regulatory reference whatever its header says."* This is a value-based test — the exact class of bug this whole project has been removing from the CODE — except here it is still written directly into the model's extraction INSTRUCTIONS, so the model correctly (per its instructions) treats AEROSIL's own genuine result column as a regulatory reference and reports it as `regulatory_limit`, discarding it as the material's content.

**Fix.** Rewrite Section E2 of `make_ei_prompt` so the test is decided by the column's LABEL/HEADER, never by whether its values match Option-1:
- A column headed with a guideline/standard name ("ICH Q3D", "Option 1 Limit", "Permitted Concentration", "USP <232> Limit", "PDE") is the regulatory reference — regardless of what values it contains.
- A column headed with a product/material/grade name (e.g. "AEROSIL® 200 Pharma", a CoA batch column, a vendor's own result column) is the material's own result — even when every one of its values happens to equal the Option-1 concentration, because a vendor legitimately controlling at the guideline limit is normal and must be kept as content.
- Explicitly instruct: "One or two elements matching Option-1 is normal and never disqualifies a column. A column whose HEADER names the material/product, not a guideline, is the material's result even if ALL of its values equal Option-1 — do not use value-matching as the test."

**Why this cannot break other documents.** This does not remove the reference-column detection — it only changes its trigger from "do the values match" to "does the header say guideline/limit." A genuine reference column (headed "ICH Q3D Option 1 Limits" or similar) is still caught. Only a genuinely product-labelled result column is now correctly kept, which is the case that was being wrongly discarded.

**Verify.** Re-run Aerosil (both Evonik documents in the Sitagliptin set); confirm Pb/As/Hg/Co/V/Ni now read their stated `<X` bounds (0.5/1.5/3/5/10/20) as the material's own evidence, not zero. Re-run Trilaciclib to confirm its API spec (which also sits at several ICH limits) is unaffected — still fully kept.

