## TICKET 3 (P0) — Fujicalin all-zero: a single material was split into two "scopes" because its name is spelled two ways across pages

**Confirmed root cause, directly from your own POST-CLUSTER log:**
```python
('Fujicalin® SG (Anhydrous Dibasic Calcium Phosphate (USP), Calcium Hydrogen Phosphate (EP))',
 False,
 ['Fujicalin SG (Anhydrous Dibasic Calcium Phosphate (USP), Calcium Hydrogen Phosphate (EP))',
  'Fujicalin SG'])
```
The third field is `_ei_material_scopes(ann) or None` — and it is a list of TWO entries, not `None`. Fujicalin is a genuinely single-material document, but it is now being treated as covering two materials.

**Why.** `_ei_material_scopes` collects names from `covered_materials` (populated by the A3 fix, per-page, from any page that names the product) plus any per-element `material_scope`. Page 1 of the Fujicalin PDF names the product in full; a later page names it in the short form "Fujicalin SG". `_ei_scope_key` only strips non-alphanumerics for comparison — it has no containment/substring logic, so "Fujicalin SG" and "Fujicalin SG (Anhydrous Dibasic Calcium Phosphate...)" are treated as two DIFFERENT materials. `_ei_material_views` then splits Fujicalin's one element list (Pb/Cd/V genuinely detected) across two views via `_ei_elements_for_scope`, and depending on which split view the resolver's matcher retrieves, the real data can end up filed under the "wrong" scope key and never reach the calculation — producing the observed all-zero despite the converter itself reporting 3 non-zero elements.

This is a direct, foreseeable side effect of the A3 fix (which correctly enabled Croscarmellose's genuinely separate section): the fix has no way, as originally written, to tell "two real different products" apart from "one product whose own name is abbreviated on a later page."

**Fix.** In `_ei_material_scopes`, after building the `distinct` dict keyed by `_ei_scope_key`, add a second, containment-based merge pass: for every pair of candidate scope names, if one candidate's normalized key is a SUBSTRING of the other's (after the same `_ei_scope_key` normalization), merge them into the LONGER (more specific) name and drop the shorter as a duplicate reference to the same material. Only names that remain genuinely distinct after this containment check count as separate covered materials. This reuses the exact containment-scoring principle `_candidate_score` (canonical-core / distinctive-token containment) already applies elsewhere in this file for ingredient identity — it is not a new rule, it is the existing rule applied at the scope-deduplication step where it was missing.

**Why this cannot break the Croscarmellose case.** "Microcrystalline Cellulose" and "Croscarmellose Sodium" are NOT substrings of each other under any normalization — they share no containment relationship, so the new merge pass leaves them exactly as two distinct scopes, unchanged.

**Verify.** Re-run Fujicalin; confirm `_ei_material_scopes` now returns `[]` (single material, no artificial split) and Table 3/6/7 show Pb=0.1, Cd=0.4, V=0.7 (or the source's actual bounds) as non-zero, matching the converter's own reported "3 non-zero" count. Re-run the IFF PRDS; confirm `_ei_material_scopes` still returns exactly 2 entries (Microcrystalline Cellulose, Croscarmellose Sodium) and Croscarmellose remains correctly matched, not reverted to unmatched.

Add a permanent regression assertion:
```python
assert _ei_material_scopes(fujicalin_annexure) == []
assert len(_ei_material_scopes(iff_annexure)) == 2
```

