## TICKET 4 (P0) — Sitagliptin API (and any two-column result-vs-limit document with NO narrative sentence): still using the Option-1 limit column, not the observed-result column

**Confirmed root cause, from the actual Jiuzhou source (`17 Elemental impurities.pdf`).** The document has TWO separate columns:
- "Concentration/Limit in ppm" — reads 0.5, 1.5, 0.5, 3, 5, 10, 20, 300 for Pb/As/Cd/Hg/Co/V/Ni/Cu. This is EXACTLY the ICH Q3D Option-1 table.
- "Observed levels or Range" — reads `<0.15, <0.45, <0.90, <1.5, <3, <6, <90`. This is the material's real result.

The generated Sitagliptin doc still shows 0.5/1.5/0.5/3/5/10/20/300 — the limit column — not the observed-result column.

**Why this is a DIFFERENT bug from Ticket 2 (Aerosil), and Ticket 2 alone will not fix it.** Ticket 2 corrects the case where a genuine result column happens to equal Option-1 (so value-matching wrongly excludes a real result). Sitagliptin's case is the mirror: a genuine result column exists WITH DIFFERENT VALUES than Option-1, but extraction is not finding/using it at all, and is defaulting to the limit column instead. The earlier attempted fix for this (a whole-column test paired with narrative prose like "not detected above the reporting limit") does not fire here because this specific document is purely tabular — it has no narrative sentence for the model to key off. The 0.5/1.5/... values are also not being caught by the Ticket-2 header-label fix either, since here the LIMIT column genuinely is headed as a limit ("Concentration/Limit in ppm") — that part is being read correctly as a limit-labelled column; the bug is that its value is landing in `concentration_value`/content in addition to (or instead of) the separately-headed result column being used.

**Fix.** Add an explicit instruction to `make_ei_prompt` (Section E, alongside the existing header-label rule from Ticket 2): "When a table prints BOTH a limit/reference column (headed 'Concentration/Limit', 'Limit', 'Option 1', 'Permitted Concentration', 'ICH Q3D', or similar) AND a SEPARATE column headed as an observed/result/range column ('Observed levels', 'Observed Range', 'Result', 'Measured', 'Content'), the OBSERVED/RESULT column's value is the material's content for `concentration_value`/`observed_level`, and the limit/reference column's value goes to `regulatory_limit` ONLY — regardless of whether any narrative sentence is present. Do not require accompanying prose to make this determination; column headers alone are sufficient and authoritative." This applies the identical header-based (never value-based, never prose-dependent) principle as Ticket 2, extended to the case where BOTH columns are present side by side in one table with no supporting narrative.

**Why this cannot break other documents.** This only activates when a table has two DISTINCTLY HEADED numeric columns for the same elements. A document with only one column (no separate result vs. limit split) is entirely unaffected — there is nothing for this rule to act on. A document that already has a narrative sentence (the earlier heuristic) is also unaffected; this is an additional, independent trigger, not a replacement.

**Verify.** Re-run the Sitagliptin API annexure; confirm the API row now shows the observed-result values (`<0.15, <0.45, <0.90, <1.5, <3, <6, <90` or their conservative_bound equivalents), NOT 0.5/1.5/0.5/3/5/10/20/300. Confirm Ni/V/Cu totals are no longer dominated by the fabricated limit-table row. Re-run Trilaciclib and Aerosil (Ticket 2's case) to confirm both remain correct — Trilaciclib's API spec (single column, no separate limit/result split) is unaffected by this new two-column rule; Aerosil's header-label fix from Ticket 2 is unaffected by this addition.

